> "ECON:," for example, accounts for 10% of all messages, then Ray only saves
> a small amount on time--less than 10% because he wouldn't likely read in
> detail a message of no interest to him.)

   You're right, lately the explosion of new prefixes has caused me to
delete a lot more. One prefix did do a lot of good. I deleted WACO:
who turned about to be about 20 msgs.

   If the majority of the people feel as Tim do, that prefixes are
unneccessary then please stop using them. They are purely voluntary (except
for META, POLL, PLEDGE, and these are mainly for drawing attention to
Harry/Max's posts).  If prefix usage dies off, "the market has spoken"
and I will stop advocating them. The final decision is up to Harry though.

  I believe the general prefixes started when Betsy Schwartz (It's all
her fault! ;-p )threatened to resign the list. She wanted a method of 
filtering out political messages, so people just started using POLI:.


> 
> And I'm confused about something Harry Shapiro said:
> 
> "I don't think it a big secret that we hope to allow
> messages to be subscribed to, etc. by prefix. That
> is why we would like people to use them, etc."
> 
> Huh? Are prefixes, with all their flakinesses (discussed by many), to be a
> kind of ersatz newsgroup?

  The general idea was to improve the digestification software to allow
customized digests (differing sizes, ordering of subjects,
subscribe/unsubscribe to individual threads/prefixes, filter by prefix
and author). So if you just got tired of WACO messages, you could
unsubscribe them and the digest software would do the rest.

  More elaborate schemes are also being worked on. Allow users to
rank threads and authors' performances, and have the software notify
them that they might like to subscribe a particular thread or perhaps
just tag those threads/authors messages as special requiring attention
first. Software filtering agents could be plugged into the software
containing user's preferences (a sort of expert system) which would
select messages based on lots of criteria, however this is farther off.

  Digicash methods are also being proposed which place market values
on good and bad posts creating a sort of futures market on posters.
I couldn't do justice to both of these ideas in a single post since
a couple of extropians have written entire essays on them.


