From: david director friedman <ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu>
Subject: ECON/AI&PORN:Self Owned Organizations and Victimless Child Porn

"The creator of such an organization might be able to create latent
goals, which only come into effect after the organization has grown
to a certain percentage of the world economy, or when some other
trigger was hit.  Who knows what strange creatures might thereby be
created." (Robin Hanson)

About 1972, I wrote a piece for the Population Council, a private
organization that funded research on population issues. The president
of the Council, Bernard Berelson (I think), told me that he had an
ultimate solution to population problems. He had extrapolated the
employed population of the world and the number of employees of the
Population Council (which was growing pretty rapidly at that point),
and calculated the date at which the two lines crossed--I think
sometime in the early 22nd century. He was leaving a memo for his
successor as of that date to circulate, if population was still a
problem. It was addressed to all employees of the Population Council
and read: "Stop having children or will terminate your employment."

Robin also asked about why such self-owned organizations had not come
into existence. I think one could make a good case for some
religions, such as Catholicism and Mormonism, fitting at least
roughly into his pattern. 

A number of people asked about the legal status of computer generated
child porn, and other forms that did not involve the use of children.
I put the question to one of my colleagues who moonlights as a
Federal judge. He showed me the relevant statute. It quite explicitly
applies only to pictures that were produced by having minors engage
in sexual acts, so the various cases discussed, from morphing frogs
to very young-looking adult actors, would clearly not be covered.
Incidentally, the statute explicitly does include computers as one of
the ways in which such pictures might be transmitted.

I spoke to him before reading the posting that discussed the ninth
circuit opinion, so I did not ask about that.

David Friedman

