From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: SCI: (was unsubscribe)

Ray Cromwell speculates:
>     IMHO,(and I am no expert on Quantum Interpretation), the whole 
> notion of wavefunction "collapse" is wrong. It gives the appearance
> that the wavefunction is "real" in a sense. The Many-Worlds
> Interpretion is more appealing because it does away with the collapse
> postulate. 

I would rather put it another way:  many-worlds makes the wavefunction
real.  This is why it can't superluminally collapse.  But many-worlds
allows the subjective appearance of collapse (relative to each
observer).
> 
> *blue-sky mode*
>     I would speculate that an observation "collapses" the wavefunction
> once it becomes "non-erasable". Once information from the result of
> an observation becomes irreversibly transmitted, the wave function is
> irreversibly collapsed. The second law and information theory probably
> has some deep connections with collapse.
> *blue-sky off*

This is my understanding too.  Release of entropy triggers the 
"collapse" of the wavefunction.  Any process that releases entropy is
irreversible and can't be undone (like the breaking of Humpty-Dumpty). 
An device coming into contact with the entropic results can treat the
results as being in a mixture of states, each state's probability given
by the conventional rules of QM.

Deutsch's reversible quantum computers screw up the conventional
interpretations of QM because they don't collapse wavefunctions (because
they're reversible), but they may be conscious.  Poor old Copenhagen
can't cope with reversible observers!

