DEAD WRONG PESSIMISTS:

        "In my opinion such a thing is impossible for many
years...[P]eople...have been talking about a 3000 miles high-
angle rocket shot from one continent to another, carrying an
atomic bomb and so directed as to be a precise weapon...I
think we can leave that out of our thinking." Dr. Vannevar
Bush, testimony to Senate committee, 1945.

"[Lee] De Forest has said in many newspapers and over his
signature that it would be possible to transmit the human
voice across the atlantic before many years. Based on these
absurd and deliberately misleading statements, the
misguided public...has been persuaded to buy stock in his
company." U.S. District Attorney, at the mail fraud trial of
Lee De Forest, "Father of Radio," 1913.

"While theoretically and technically television may be
feasible, commmercially and financially I consider it an
impossibility, a development of which we need waste little
time dreaming." Lee De Forest, "Father of the Radio", 1926.

"We hope that Professor Langley will not put his substantial
greatness as a scientist in further peril by continuing to
waste his time, and the money involved, in further airship
experiments. Life is short, and he is capable of services to
humanity incomparably greater than can be expected to
result from trying to fly...For students and investigators of
the Langley type there are more useful employments." The
New York Times, December 10 1903. [The first 852 feet long
flight of a powered heavier than air machne took place on
December 17!]

Counterpoint: "Tout ce qu'un homme est capable d'imaginer,
d'autres hommes seront capable de la realiser." Jules Verne.

"That Professor Goddard and his "chair" in Clark College and
the countenancing of the Smithsonian Institution does not
know the relation of action and reaction and of the need to
have something better than a vacuum against which to react
- to say that would be absurd. Of course he only seems to
lack the knowledge ladled out daily in high schools." The
New York Times, 13 January 1920.

"Landing and moving around the moon offers so many
serious problems for human beings that it may take science
another 200 years to lick them." Science Digest, August
1948.

"What can be palpably absurd than the prospect held out of
locomotives travelling twice as fast as stagecoaches?" The
Quarterly Review, 1825.

"The ordinary "horseless carriage" is at present a luxury for
the wealthy; and although its price will probably fall in the
future, it will never, of course, come into as common use as
the bicycle." The Literary Digest, 14 October 1889.

"The popular mind often pictures gigantic flying machines
speeding across the Atlantic and carrying innumerable
passengers...[E]ven if a machine could get across with one or
two passengers the expense would be prohibitive...Another
popular fallacy is to expect enormous speed....The resistance
of the air increases as the square of the speed and the work
as the cube...It is clear that with our present devices there is
no hope of competing for racing speed with either our
locomotives or our automobiles." William H. Pickering, US
Astronomer, c. 1910.

"The actual building of roads devoted to motor cars is not for
the near future, in spite of many rumours to that effect."
Harper's Weekly, August 2, 1902.

"There is no plea which will justify the use of high tension
and alternating currents, either in a scientific or a
commercial sense." Thomas A. Edison, 1889.

I must confess that in my imagination, in spite even of
spurring, refuses to see any sort of submarine doing
anything but suffocating its crew and floundering at sea."
H.G. Wells, in Anticipations, 1901.

"The energy produced by the breaking down of the atom is a
very poor kind of thing. Anyone who expects a source of
power from the transformations of these atoms is talking
moonshine." Ernest Rutherford, physicist, ca. 1930.


