No, professors present a "mixture" of views because the interpretation of
quantum mechanics is in fact in a "mixed state" until a measurement is
made!

Hope this helps.

-Tim

(This was meant as a joke, but in writing it I realized it's not all that
far from being true. The various interpretations, ably discussed here by
you all, are in fact largely indistinguishable from each other--they are
not "falsifiable" in the Popperian sense we like so much. John Cramer's
"transactional model" (which I can't claim to have studied enough to
comment on) may be testable, he thinks, under just the right circumstances.
I don't know. Some think cosmological details, such as the ratio of matter
to antimatter or the properties of the very early universe, may tell us if
the Many Worlds interpretation is plausible. I won't hold my breath. By and
large, the differing interpretations are likely to persist for a while.)


