Date: Wed, 10 Mar 93 13:24:15 GMT
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: META: No More Prefixes!

I would be unlikely to subscribe to only a subset of this list.  However, I
do find the prefixes useful, for several reasons:


1.  It helps to differentiate mailing-list mail from personal email. 
Extropians is not the only list to adopt prefixes, and I'd like to see the
practice catch on even more.


2.  They provide a forewarning of what the topic of the message is.  Even
if I'm going to read the message anyway, I find this useful.


3.  Sorting my mail by subject brings related messages together, even when
they are only loosely related, by having the same prefix.  I find that this
speeds up the rate at which I can deal with them.


4.  Timothy May suggested that no information would be lost by dropping
prefixes.  Even for the hypothetical examples he gave, I don't think this
is entirely true.  It's more effort to get the information from a long
subject line than from a short all-caps prefix.  And for real examples,
it's even less true.  "QUANTUM: Suggestion" is the worst offender in my
mailbox today.


5.  Tim May writes:
>[This version needs no prefix, as it is intended for those who look at the
>title of a message, and perhasp glance at the content, before deciding
>whether to discard it.]

The prefix *is* part of the title.  See (4).  Saying that people should use
better titles begs the question.  Using prefixes *is* a way of improving
titles.


6.  I use Eudora to receive my mail, the same as Tim May does.  Although in
its current version it does not have any automatic mail-sorting capability,
such a feature might be present in a future version, based on
pattern-matching of the header and body of incoming messages.  (But don't
tell the author I said so!  Expect it when you see it, not before.) 
Prefixes would facilitate this process in an obvious way.

